---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { site } from '../../lib/site';
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const faqs = (await getCollection('faqs')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const siteUrl = (Astro.site ?? Astro.url).origin;
const path = '/guides/incorporate-in-india';

const howTo = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: 'How to incorporate a startup in India',
  description:
    'Step-by-step guide for Indian founders to choose an entity, reserve a name, file SPICe+, and complete first-year compliance.',
  inLanguage: site.language,
  supply: [{ '@type': 'HowToSupply', name: 'Director KYC documents, registered office proof, and digital signatures' }],
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: 'Choose the entity', text: 'Pick sole prop, LLP, OPC, or Private Limited based on capital, co-founders, and whether you will raise.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: 'Get DSC and DIN', text: 'Directors need a digital signature and Director Identification Number before SPICe+.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: 'Reserve the name', text: 'File SPICe+ Part A with backup names that do not clash with trademarks or existing companies.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: 'File incorporation', text: 'Complete SPICe+ Part B with MOA, AOA, office proof, and optional PAN, TAN, EPFO, ESIC, and GST.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 5, name: 'Open the bank account and issue shares', text: 'Deposit subscription money, issue share certificates, and hold the first board meeting.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 6, name: 'Register what you actually need next', text: 'GST, Udyam, and DPIIT recognition if they help customers, banks, or schemes — not as decoration.' },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="How to Incorporate a Startup in India (2026 Guide)"
  description="A 2026 guide for Indian founders: Pvt Ltd vs LLP vs OPC, SPICe+ incorporation, GST, DPIIT recognition, costs, NRI directors, and first-year ROC compliance."
  type="article"
  keywords={[
    'how to incorporate a startup in India',
    'register Private Limited company India',
    'SPICe+ company registration',
    'Pvt Ltd vs LLP 2026',
    'Startup India registration',
    'DPIIT recognition',
  ]}
  jsonLd={[
    breadcrumbSchema(siteUrl, [
      { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/incorporate-in-india' },
      { name: 'Incorporate a startup in India', path: path },
    ]),
    articleSchema({
      siteUrl,
      title: 'How to incorporate a startup in India (2026)',
      description: 'Entity choice, SPICe+, GST, DPIIT, and first-year compliance for Indian founders.',
      path,
      type: 'TechArticle',
    }),
    howTo,
  ]}
>
  <article class="container guide">
    <p class="eyebrow">Free guide · Updated 2026</p>
    <h1>How to incorporate a startup in India</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      If you are a first-time founder in India, the legal wrapper is not the company — but getting it wrong will stall
      a bank account, a GST invoice, or a term sheet. This is the sequence most product and services startups actually follow.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#entity">Pick an entity</a></li>
        <li><a href="#steps">Incorporate a Pvt Ltd</a></li>
        <li><a href="#after">After the CIN arrives</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cost">What it costs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">Detailed FAQ</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="entity">
      <h2>1. Pick the entity before you pick the logo</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Private Limited</strong> is the default if you will raise capital, issue ESOPs, or hire at any scale.
        Indian VCs almost never close into an LLP. An <strong>OPC</strong> works for a solo founder who wants limited
        liability now and can convert later. An <strong>LLP</strong> fits professional firms that will not sell shares.
        A <strong>sole proprietorship</strong> is fine while you are testing a service with your own money.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the full comparison: <a href="/faq/which-entity">Pvt Ltd vs LLP vs OPC</a> and
        <a href="/faq/pvt-ltd-vs-llp-vcs">why VCs insist on a Pvt Ltd</a>.
        You can <a href="/faq/convert-entity">convert later</a>, but it is slower than starting with the right form.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="steps">
      <h2>2. How a Private Limited actually gets registered</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>DSC and DIN.</strong> Every proposed director needs a digital signature. DIN is allotted during SPICe+ if they do not already have one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Name reservation (SPICe+ Part A).</strong> Avoid names too close to a trademark or an existing company. Keep two backups.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Incorporation (SPICe+ Part B).</strong> MOA, AOA, registered-office proof, and KYC. PAN, TAN, EPFO, ESIC, and sometimes GST can ride along.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Certificate of Incorporation.</strong> You now have a CIN. Open a current account, deposit share capital, issue certificates.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Step-by-step with forms: <a href="/faq/how-to-incorporate">How to reserve a name and incorporate</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="after">
      <h2>3. After you have a CIN — do not stop</h2>
      <p>
        First board meeting, first auditor, statutory registers, and a calendar for AOC-4 / MGT-7.
        <a href="/faq/first-year-compliance">First-year Pvt Ltd compliance</a> is the list investors will ask about in diligence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Register <a href="/faq/gst-registration">GST</a> when you hit the threshold, sell inter-state, or a customer refuses to pay without a GSTIN.
        Apply for <a href="/faq/dpiit-recognition">DPIIT recognition</a> if you want Startup India schemes or a cleaner conversation with banks.
        <a href="/faq/udyam-msme">Udyam</a> is free and often requested for MSME-linked credit.
      </p>
      <p>
        NRIs and foreign co-founders: you still need one resident director, and share allotment is FDI paperwork.
        See <a href="/faq/nri-founder">Can an NRI be a founder?</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cost">
      <h2>4. What founders actually budget</h2>
      <p>
        Government fees are usually modest. Professional fees plus first-year audit and bookkeeping are the real number.
        <a href="/faq/pvt-ltd-cost">Typical incorporation cost</a> for a simple two-director company.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="faq">
      <h2>5. Every incorporation question on this site</h2>
      <ul class="faq-index">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <li><a href={`/faq/${faq.id}`}>{faq.data.question}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="disclaimer">
      This guide is for education. MCA fees, GST thresholds, and FEMA rules change. Confirm the current position with a CA or company secretary before you file.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide {
    max-width: 800px;
    padding: 3.5rem 0 4rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--accent-color);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .toc {
    background: var(--card-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .toc p {
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .toc ol {
    margin-left: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .toc li {
    margin: 0.3rem 0;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.45rem;
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0.9rem;
  }

  p, .steps, .faq-index {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .steps {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  .steps li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .faq-index {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.55rem;
  }

  .faq-index a,
  .toc a {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .disclaimer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
</style>
